{"outcome": true, "goal": "  subject: high_school_physics\n  question: Standing waves are produced by a 100-Hz generator in a string of fixed length. The tension in the string is increased until a new set of standing waves is produced. Will the wavelength of the new standing waves be greater than or less than the wavelength of the original standing waves?\n  do not use query-web or semantic-scholar in your reasoning. use generate or think to surface knownledge, or refine to extract information from text.\n  choices:\n    A: Less, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies inversely with the wavelength.\n    B: Greater, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies inversely with the wavelength.\n    C: Greater, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies directly with the wavelength.\n    D: Less, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies directly with the wavelength.\n\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\noutput your answer in this format:\nANSWER: X\nwhere X is A, B, C, or D.\nFinally, say the answer to User: ", "plan": [{"type": "generate-note", "prompt": "Explain the relationship between wave speed, tension, linear density, frequency, and wavelength for standing waves on a string. Include the relevant physics formulas.", "out": "$physics_explanation"}, {"type": "think", "value": "From the generated explanation, I know that: 1) Wave speed v = √(T/μ), where T is tension and μ is linear density. 2) For standing waves, v = fλ, where f is frequency and λ is wavelength. 3) The frequency f is fixed at 100 Hz. 4) The string length is fixed.  If T increases: - v increases (since v = √(T/μ) and T is in the numerator) - Since v = fλ and f is constant, if v increases, then λ must also increase to maintain the equality. - Therefore, the wavelength of the new standing waves will be greater than the original wavelength. The correct relationship is: T increases → v increases → λ increases (since f is constant)."}, {"type": "generate-note", "prompt": "Analyze the following answer choices for the physics problem about standing waves and tension. Which choice correctly explains that when tension increases in a string with fixed frequency, the wavelength of the standing waves increases? Choice A: Less, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies inversely with the wavelength. Choice B: Greater, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies inversely with the wavelength. Choice C: Greater, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies directly with the wavelength. Choice D: Less, because the tension in the string varies directly with the wave speed, which varies directly with the wavelength.", "out": "$choice_analysis"}], "error_count": 0}
