ixed at 100 Hz. 4) The string length is fixed.  If T increases: - v increases (since v = √(T/μ) and T is in the numerator) - Since v = fλ and f is constant, if v increases, then λ must also increase to maintain the equality. - Therefore, the wavelength of the new standing waves will be greater than the original wavelength. The correct relationship is: T increases → v increases → λ increases (since f is constant). Now I need to check the answer choices to see which one matches this logic. Choice A says 'Less' and 'varies inversely with wavelength' - this is wrong because wavelength increases. Choice B says 'Greater' and 'varies inversely with wavelength' - this is wrong because wavelength varies directly with speed, not inversely. Choice C says 'Greater' and 'varies directly with wavelength' - this is correct because wavelength increases with speed. Choice D says 'Less' and 'varies directly with wavelength' - this is wrong because wavelength increases, not decreases."}], "error_count": 0}
